.he "rcp (1)                      10/14/26                     rcp (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
rcp
- copy files to or from a remote host, over rsh
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd 2
rcp [ -r ] [ -P port ] host:file ... dir
rcp [ -r ] [ -P port ] file ... host:dir
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Rcp moves files over the network card (sys/NET.md), speaking rsh to the
remote's rshd and the rcp file stream over that one connection.  One
side is remote and carries the host: prefix (an ip address); the other
is local.  The last argument is the destination: a directory when there
is more than one source or it ends in /, into which each source lands
under its basename; a single source into a non-slash destination is
renamed to it.
.sp
There is no password: the remote trusts this host (.rhosts).
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-r	Copy directories recursively.

.ti -10
-P port	Use port as the rshd port; the default is 514.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
GLOBBING
.in +5
.br
Rcp does not expand filename patterns itself.  A remote path is split at
the colon, and the part after it is passed on to the remote rcp -f or
rcp -t command; the remote's rshd runs that command through the login
shell, which expands *, ? and [..] against the remote filesystem and
then runs rcp with the result.  Thus
.sp
rcp nook:/usr/src/*.c /tmp
.sp
copies every file matching the pattern, not a file named *.c.
.sp
Quote the remote path to keep the local shell from expanding it first;
either way the pattern reaches the remote.  Because the remote shell
expands metacharacters, there is no way to copy a remote file whose own
name is one of them: the shell expands it before rcp ever sees it.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
net (4)
